  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 302 AM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nToday will be the last day of unseasonably warm temperatures (10-15 \ndegrees above average) as influence from the 500mb jet stream brings \nus into a pattern change. The jet is continuing to dig southward and \nas a result, an associated low pressure center (currently centered \nover west-central Iowa as of 09z) will continue to progress \nsoutheastward into central Illinois by late morning. As a result, a \ncold front will pass through Missouri during the morning hours, \nwhich will result in a wind shift from southwest to northwest. \nTemperatures will still be able to climb into the 60s as the \ndynamics will allow for enhanced mixing. With the low pressure \nsystem to our east, the area will see a pressure gradient, which \nwill result in wind speeds increasing to 15-20kts, with gusts \napproaching or slightly exceeding 30kts. Winds die down in the \nevening as the low continues to progress to the east. During the \nWednesday night into Thursday morning time frame, a secondary cold \nfront the north will progress into our region. This will allow for \ncold air advection to increase overnight and throughout the day \nThursday. Because of this cold surge, highs on Thursday are only \nexpected to get up into the mid 40s to low 50s.\n\nBy the time we get to Thursday night into Friday morning, skies will \nbe mostly clear, with higher concentrations of clouds in eastern \nMissouri. This will allow for much of the state to see temperatures \nbelow freezing, getting down to the mid 20s. Since the \nclimatological date for our first hard-freeze has passed, any type \nof issuance to reflect a frost/freeze headline will not be issued. \nRegardless, any sensitive plants will need to be protected. The \ntrough will continue to dig, and temperatures will remain in the 40s \nas the jet will keep milder temperatures to our south. Temperatures \nbelow freezing are expected overnight Friday into Saturday Morning.\n\nThe weather will remain quiet during the day on Saturday as we \ncontinue to see influence from our airmass from the north with \nresponse from the jet stream and surface high pressure north of Lake \nSuperior. By the middle of the weekend, the pattern becomes much \nmore zonal, but a shortwave may bring the return of rain on Sunday \ninto Monday. Highest chances remain to our south and west, but exact \ntotals and spatial coverage for our area will become more clear as \nwe get closer to the weekend.",
